Brute Force [Hash Map]
Approach : 
Create temp pointer pointing to head
Create unordered_map to store visited or not 
if again visited to same node then return temp
Add it to map 
If no loop present return null

Time Complexity : O(N)
Space Complexity : O(N)
Additional space used by hash map

Code : 

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode *findStartingPoint(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* temp=head;
        std::unordered_map<ListNode*, int> mp;
        while(temp!=NULL){
           if( mp.count(temp)!=0){
            return temp;
           }
           mp[temp]=1;
           temp=temp->next;
        }
        return NULL;

    }
};

Brute Force [Hash Set]
Approach:
Create a temp pointer pointing to head.
Create an unordered_set to store the visited nodes.
Traverse the linked list.
If the current node is already present in the set, return temp because it is the starting point of the loop.
Otherwise, insert the current node into the set.
Move temp to the next node.
If no loop is present, return NULL.

Time Complexity: O(N)

Traverse the linked list once.
unordered_set insertion and search take O(1) on average.

Space Complexity: O(N)

Additional space is used by the hash set to store all visited nodes.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode *findStartingPoint(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* temp = head;
        unordered_set<ListNode*> st;

        while (temp != NULL) {
            if (st.count(temp) != 0) {
                return temp;
            }

            st.insert(temp);
            temp = temp->next;
        }

        return NULL;
    }
};

Floyd's Algorithm [Tortoise and Hare Algorithm]

Approach:

Create two pointers slow and fast pointing to head.
slow moves one node at a time.
fast moves two nodes at a time.
Traverse the linked list until fast is not NULL and fast->next is not NULL.
If slow and fast meet, it means a loop is present.
Move slow back to head.
Now move both slow and fast one node at a time.
The node where they meet again is the starting point of the loop.
Return that node.
If no loop is present, return NULL.

Time Complexity: O(N)

Traverse the linked list once to detect the loop.
Again move both pointers to find the starting point.
Overall time complexity is O(N).

Space Complexity: O(1)

Only two pointers are used.
No extra space is required.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode *findStartingPoint(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;

            if (slow == fast) {
                slow = head;

                while (slow != fast) {
                    slow = slow->next;
                    fast = fast->next;
                }

                return slow;
            }
        }

        return NULL;
    }
};

//IMPORTANT 

Why do we move slow back to head?

Suppose,

L1 = Distance from head to the starting point of the loop.
d = Distance from the starting point of the loop to the meeting point of slow and fast.
LoopLength = Total number of nodes in the loop.

When slow and fast meet,

slow has travelled L1 + d.
fast has travelled 2(L1 + d) because it moves twice as fast.

Also,

Fast Distance = Slow Distance + LoopLength

So,

2(L1 + d) = (L1 + d) + LoopLength
L1 + d = LoopLength
L1 = LoopLength - d

This means:

Distance from head to the starting point of the loop = L1.
Distance from the meeting point to the starting point of the loop = LoopLength - d.

Both distances are equal.

So, after detecting the loop:

Move slow back to head.
Keep fast at the meeting point.
Move both one node at a time.
The node where they meet again is the starting point of the loop.
 
